NAME
    vnu - report what system you are running on

SYNOPSIS
    vnu [COMMAND]...

DESCRIPTION
    Prints a summary of the running system: its versions, how long it
    has been up, its host name, memory use, the shell and file system
    the session runs in, the display, and how much space each part of
    the system image takes.

    Every value is read from the running system (uname(2), gfx_getinfo(2)
    for the display, the /proc files listed below, and the size
    accounting in the kernel, so the output describes the machine you
    are on, not a built-in table.
    Nothing is written to disk and no other process is disturbed, so
    vnu is safe to run at any time, including from scripts.

    With no COMMAND the usage summary is printed.

COMMANDS
    fetch     overview of the running system: OS and kernel version,
              uptime, host name, memory, shell, file system, display
              with its depth and driver, compiler version and build
              date
    version   version block: OS, kernel, compiler, ABI version and
              build date
    size      image size per component (kernel, userspace, libraries,
              fonts, resources) with the number of objects in each
    set       read or flip the outside-access switch, see 'vnu set'
              below: outsideaccess on starts the SSH daemon, off stops it
    install   install VNU onto a disk, see 'vnu install' below

    The remaining options are:

        --help    display this help and exit
        --version output version information and exit

VNU SET
    set outsideaccess    print whether remote login is allowed
    set outsideaccess on     allow it: record 'outsideaccess on' in
        /etc/vnu/config/vsshd.conf and start the SSH daemon vsshd(1)
    set outsideaccess off    forbid it: flip the config file and stop
        the daemon (it notices the flip within a second and exits;
        SIGTERM to the pid in /tmp/vsshd.pid is only the fast path)

    Turning outside access on forks /bin/vsshd, which binds port 22 and
    accepts SSH-2 connections: RSA host key, diffie-hellman-group14
    key exchange, password login against /etc/passwd, and on success a
    session running /bin/vash. A second 'vnu set outsideaccess on' finds
    the port taken and leaves the running daemon alone.

VNU INSTALL
    With no argument, install opens a full-screen wizard in the style of
    the classic Windows Setup / FreeBSD sysinstall / Slackware setup
    screens: pick the target disk (from /proc/disks), choose the
    partition size, type the host name for the machine, review the
    choices, confirm the destructive write and reboot. Blue screens use
    the console's ANSI color support.

    With arguments it runs without asking anything:

        vnu install DRIVE HOSTNAME [SIZE_MIB]

    DRIVE is the disk index (0..N-1, the same numbering as /proc/disks),
    HOSTNAME the name the machine should boot with (see 'man
    hostname'; it is written to /etc/hostname and recorded on the disk)
    and SIZE_MIB the size of the boot partition in MiB, omitted to split
    the usable disk in half. Requires root.

    The write itself is done by the kernel (syscall VNU_SYS_install,
    30): it stamps the MBR + GRUB core image, records the host name in
    the config sector before the partition, and creates a FAT16
    partition at LBA 2048 holding /boot/kernel.elf and a GRUB config, so
    the disk boots on its own with no CD. This is what a distro
    'sysinst' does.

    A second FAT16 partition follows the boot one. It is the data
    volume, labelled VNUDATA, and the kernel mounts it at /mnt when the
    disk boots, so files written under /mnt survive a reboot. The home
    directories and the system settings live there too: /home, /root
    and /etc are served from the volume's home, root and etc subtrees
    (seeded from the RAM defaults on a fresh disk), so user files and
    configuration survive a reboot while the rest of the root stays a
    RAM file system. The boot partition takes SIZE_MIB, or half the
    usable disk when SIZE_MIB is omitted; everything left is the data
    volume, and a disk too small for both gets only the boot volume.
    /mnt holds ordinary files and directories, but they are FAT16: they
    carry no owner and no permission bits, so chmod and chown on them
    do nothing, and names follow the FAT16 rules. The volumes appear in
    /proc/mounts and the block devices behind them under /dev (hda,
    hda1, hda2, ...).

EXAMPLES
    vnu
    vnu fetch      the Graphics line: driver, WxH and depth
    vnu version
    vnu size
    vnu set outsideaccess    is remote login allowed?
    vnu set outsideaccess on start the SSH daemon
    vnu install           the wizard
    vnu install 0 mybox   disk 0, named mybox, split half boot / half /mnt

FILES
    /proc/version  OS, kernel and build versions
    /proc/meminfo  total and free memory
    /proc/boot     root file system, install state, session shell
    /proc/images   per-component image sizes
    /etc/hostname  the name of this machine
    /etc/vnu/config/vsshd.conf  the outside-access switch
    /tmp/vsshd.pid  the pid of the running SSH daemon

EXIT STATUS
    0 on success, 1 when the requested information cannot be read, or
    when an install fails (in which case nothing was written to the
    disk).
